# hostile plan: seed=0 ops=4
#
# THE FINDING, and now the regression that guards its fix. Found at HEAD
# (8c3bc64) by this exerciser on 2026-08-21, filed under "hostile-agent"
# in docs/TODO.md, fixed under "modebits-agent".
#
# A file chmod'd 0444 accepted a write through the NFS frontend. Through
# raw FUSE the same write was refused with EACCES, because the mount asks
# for `default_permissions` and the kernel applies the ordinary mode check
# before it sends anything to a FUSE server. NFSv3 puts that check on the
# SERVER, and go-nfs's handlers and internal/vfsbilly consulted no mode
# bit, so nothing performed it: an unprivileged process wrote a file it
# had no write permission on and the bytes landed -- they survived the
# seal and read back from the sealed generation.
#
#     ro.dat: byte 0 is 0x29 in the mount, 0xc2 in the reference
#
# The reference tree is what makes this a bug rather than POSIX. The
# container runs as root, and root may legitimately write a 0444 file --
# but only with CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE, and the launcher drops it
# (--cap-drop ALL --cap-add SYS_ADMIN,CHOWN,FOWNER). The tmpfs reference
# in the same container, as the same process, REFUSED this write. So did
# the kernel's FUSE check. Only the NFS frontend allowed it.
#
# The same shape with `chown` diverged identically when CAP_CHOWN was
# absent (scripts/hostile-docker.sh --drop-chown). One cause, two
# symptoms: the NFS frontend enforced no permission checks of its own and
# got none from the client.
#
# FIXED in internal/vfsbilly (perm.go): the frontend now applies the POSIX
# model itself -- the equivalent of `default_permissions` for the path
# that has no kernel to ask -- as the server's own identity, with the
# server's own capability set, so the two frontends answer this the same
# way. The marker that made this entry expect a divergence is GONE, which
# is what the corpus discipline asks for: from here on the entry FAILS if
# the write is ever accepted again.
#
# What a passing run looks like now: the reference refuses the pwrite on
# permission grounds, the mount refuses it too, the op is recorded as
# NOT COMPARABLE, and the compare that follows finds ro.dat byte-identical
# on both sides.
create ro.dat len=256 fill=41
chmod ro.dat mode=0444 # no write permission for anyone
pwrite ro.dat off=0 len=64 fill=42 # refused on BOTH frontends now; the bytes do not land
compare
